## Explanation
In C, a "signal" is a notification sent to your program by the operating system to tell it that something important has happened. `SIGINT` stands for "Signal Interrupt." Think of it like a **fire alarm** in a building: when the alarm rings, you don't just ignore it and keep working; you stop what you are doing, save your important documents, and exit the building safely. By default, when you press `Ctrl+C` on your keyboard, the operating system sends a `SIGINT` to your program, which tells it to shut down immediately. To "handle" this signal, you write a special function that tells the program, "When you hear the alarm, run this specific cleanup code instead of just quitting."

## Use Cases
Handling `SIGINT` is crucial for programs that manage files, databases, or network connections. If a program just quits abruptly, it might leave a file half-written or a connection "hanging," which can cause data corruption. By handling `SIGINT`, you ensure the program can close files, save progress, and exit gracefully whenever the user decides to stop the process.

## Visual Model
```mermaid
graph TD
    A["Program Running"] --> B["User presses Ctrl+C"]
    B --> C["Operating System sends SIGINT"]
    C --> D["Program pauses execution"]
    D --> E["Signal Handler Function runs"]
    E --> F["Cleanup tasks performed"]
    F --> G["Program exits safely"]
```

## Example from Class
While not in your provided text, here is how you would typically set this up using the `<signal.h>` library:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// This is the "handler" function that runs when the signal happens
void handle_sigint(int sig) {
    printf("\nCaught SIGINT! Cleaning up...\n");
    exit(0);
}

int main() {
    // Tell the system to use our function when SIGINT occurs
    signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint);

    printf("Program running. Press Ctrl+C to trigger the handler.\n");
    while(1) { } // Infinite loop to keep the program alive
    return 0;
}
```

